[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Client State Management and Caching in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike traditional REST APIs where state management often relies on third-party libraries like Redux or Zustand for both server and client data, GraphQL clients manage server data caching automatically. This tutorial explores how normalized caching works in modern GraphQL clients and how to manage local client-only state efficiently."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Normalized Cache"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Popular GraphQL clients like Apollo Client and Relay use a normalized cache. Instead of storing query responses as raw JSON trees, the client flattens the data into a store of individual objects identified by a unique key, typically a combination of the object type and its ID."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automatic Updates: When a mutation updates an entity, any component querying that same entity anywhere in the application updates automatically."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deduplication: Identical objects across different queries are stored only once, reducing memory overhead."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring the Apollo Cache"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is how to initialize Apollo Client with an InMemoryCache instance, including custom type policies for unique identifier handling."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import { ApolloClient, InMemoryCache, gql } from '@client/core';

const cache = new InMemoryCache({
  typePolicies: {
    Query: {
      fields: {
        user(_, { args, toReference }) {
          return toReference({
            __typename: 'User',
            id: args.id,
          });
        },
      },
    },
  },
});

export const client = new ApolloClient({
  uri: 'https://api.example.com/graphql',
  cache,
});"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Managing Local Client State"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"GraphQL clients can also manage local state (such as UI modals, theme preferences, or active tabs) directly inside the cache using reactive variables or local fields."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import { makeVar } from '@client/core';

// Initialize a reactive variable for theme state
export const darkModeVar = makeVar(false);

// Read the variable
const currentTheme = darkModeVar();

// Update the variable
darkModeVar(true);"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Cache Eviction and Garbage Collection"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Over time, the normalized cache can accumulate stale data. Clients provide APIs like evict and gc to clean up unreferenced objects and free up memory."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Evict a specific cache entry after deletion
client.cache.evict({ id: 'User:12345' });

// Run garbage collection to remove dangling references
client.cache.gc();"}]
